/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (Despite 2025 showing a lower total attack count at the midpoint \(4.6k vs. 6.0k in 2024\), the early months paint a picture of intensifying campaigns, possibly driven by geopolitical motivations and the rise of new threat actors. The drop-off post-February suggests either enhanced mitigation efforts or a strategic pause by some actor groups. As we move into the second half of 2025, the question remains: will we see a resurgence similar to the late-year climb observed in 2024? The evolving tactics of ransomware groups, hacktivist collectives, and data brokers will play a decisive role in shaping that trajectory.)
/E (Despite 2025 showing a lower total attack count at the midpoint \(4.6k vs. 6.0k in 2024\), the early months paint a picture of intensifying campaigns, possibly driven by geopolitical motivations and the rise of new threat actors. The drop-off post-February suggests either enhanced mitigation efforts or a strategic pause by some actor groups. As we move into the second half of 2025, the question remains: will we see a resurgence similar to the late-year climb observed in 2024? The evolving tactics of ransomware groups, hacktivist collectives, and data brokers will play a decisive role in shaping that trajectory.)
/Pg 25 0 R
